WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.195 --> 00:00:02.570
STEPHANIE WONG: So hey, Ed,
now for our Hipster Shop app,

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.190
we've got some basic
services set up.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:07.072
But my site is getting so
popular, I need to do more.

00:00:07.072 --> 00:00:08.780
ED HOFMANN: Istio can
help with that too.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.510
There are all kinds of advanced
traffic management features

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.654
you can do to
control your content.

00:00:13.654 --> 00:00:14.570
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.028
So does that mean
that you're going

00:00:16.028 --> 00:00:18.800
to show me how it works before
my site becomes too mainstream?

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:19.490
ED HOFMANN: Sure thing.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:20.570
Let's jump right into it.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:24.944
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.470
So right away, we're at
Hipster Shop version one.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.250
We can scroll
through the web page.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.750
We can click on products.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.810
And we can figure out if we want
to add them to our cart or not.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.140
But right away, let's
take a step back here.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.380
Let's go back to our main page.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.810
And let's figure out
how we want to do

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.500
routing to our front ends.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.230
So first, here,
we'll run a command

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.520
to see the different front
ends that we're running.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.061
And you see right
here, we're running

00:00:53.061 --> 00:00:56.120
three different versions
of our front end.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.130
So what we'll do now
is we'll take a look

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.200
at the virtual services
that are running,

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.930
and we'll take a look to
see what the front end

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.790
virtual service
is actually doing

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.340
and how it's routing
traffic currently.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.470
So when we run
this command, we'll

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.290
see here that all
the traffic is being

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:17.870
routed to some subset of our
pods known as version one.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.570
Now what does V1 mean?

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.450
So here what we'll do is we'll
take a look at the destination

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.230
rules.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:26.780
And in particular, we'll
take a look at the front end

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.560
destination rule to
see what V1 means.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.240
When we take a look
at this, we see

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.540
that V1 relates to version
one of our application,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.780
V2 to version two, and
V3 to version three,

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.960
a nice, simple way of
keeping all this organized.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.025
So right here, we have a user
that's already on the web page.

00:01:45.025 --> 00:01:46.880
So let's take a look
at what we would

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.480
do to route this user's traffic
to version two of the web page.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.970
So this user has a
shop session cookie,

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:57.620
so we'll apply that into
our virtual service,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.630
and we'll apply this YAML file.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:02.360
And we'll go and we'll take
a look at our web page.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:03.464
We'll reload the page.

00:02:03.464 --> 00:02:05.880
And we notice now that this
user is currently being routed

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.400
to version two of the web page.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.759
So we'll take this again link.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.200
We'll go into an
incognito window.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.150
And we'll see what
other users are seeing.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.240
And you see right away
that they're being routed

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.900
to version one of the web page.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.510
So now that's great
and all, but what

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:25.300
if we want to do some canary
deployments or some 80-20

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.190
or traffic splitting between
our different versions?

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:29.720
So the next thing
we'll do is we'll

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.440
take a look at a YAML
file, which shows us

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.610
what it would look like to
spilt traffic 75% to version one

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.310
and 25% to version
three for all users

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.662
who are currently
not on the page.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.870
So right here, it's just as
simple as adding a weight

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.250
to the YAML file.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.650
So we'll go ahead
and apply this,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.110
and then we'll go back
and check on our web page.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.160
So for the user that
currently has a session ID,

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.720
we'll keep refreshing the page.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.520
And if you notice
here, it's still

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.590
being routed to version
two of the website.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.290
So now let's open
another incognito window,

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.030
and let's see what's
going on for what

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:06.210
all the other users see.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.350
So you see right away that
they're at Hipster Shop version

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.070
one.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.720
If we reload the page
a couple of times,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.510
we should see about one
in every four refresh

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.680
get routed to version
three of the web page.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.460
Now, this gives you a very
simple way to route traffic

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.850
and do some splitting
between your services.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.781
And simple enough, that was
traffic management with Istio.

00:03:28.781 --> 00:03:30.530
STEPHANIE WONG: And
it was as easy as that

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.030
to do weighted
traffic distribution

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.410
to different
versions of our app.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:34.118
ED HOFMANN: Yeah.

00:03:34.118 --> 00:03:36.590
Behind the scenes, Istio is
doing all the routing and work,

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.810
making it super
easy for dev ops.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:40.910
Don't forget to like,
comment, and subscribe

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:42.610
for our next episode on Istio.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.110
STEPHANIE WONG: And if you'd
